.side, .maincol { display: contents; }
.controls { order: 1; }
.stats { order: 2; }
.mesh-card { order: 3; }
.chart-card { order: 4; }
.queue { order: 5; }
.policy { order: 6; }
.log-card { order: 7; }
```

But `.side` and `.maincol` had gap irrelevant. Children margins none. Dashboard gap 18px. Good.

However `display: contents` on `.side` (a section) means `.side .controls { grid-row: span 2 }` from 960 rule still applies? At <=720 we set `.side .controls { grid-row: auto }` already exists. Keep.

Then stats appear after controls, before queue. That's good. But queue is far down; the target queue is important. Maybe order: controls, queue, stats, mesh, chart, policy, log. The initial queue is only 3 items and ~250px. Hmm. On mobile, seeing stats before queue might be better. Let's order: controls → stats → mesh → chart → queue → policy → log. Fine.

